    "case_id": "468",
    "case_summary": "A 7-year-old boy presented with a 2-year history of progressive eruption of papules and nodules all over his body. The lesions did not cause pain or itching. The patient had no family history of cutaneous disorders.\n\nMultiple yellow to reddish brown firm papules and nodules were present on the scalp, face, neck, trunk, and limbs, including the axillae and inguinal regions. The nasal and oropharyngeal mucosa were also affected. The lesions were 2 to 10 mm in diameter with coalescing, yellow-brown nodules and plaques up to 4 cm in diameter on the face, trunk, and limbs.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': 'Routine laboratory test results and serum chemistry with serum lipid levels were within normal ranges.', 'Imaging Studies': 'No clinical evidence of internal involvement was found on ultrasonography, computed tomography, or magnetic resonance imaging results.', 'Histopathological Examination': {'Findings': 'Dense diffuse cellular infiltrate composed mainly of foamy histiocytic cells and multinucleated Touton cells mixed with lymphocytes and eosinophils. Spindle cells and increased numbers of collagen fibers in a storiform arrangement were visible in the deep dermis.', 'Immunohistochemical Staining': 'Positive for CD68 and CD163, but negative for S100, CD1a, and Langerin.', 'Images': {'Figure 1': 'Clinical images showing disseminated, yellowish, xanthomatous maculopapular lesions and nodules before and after treatment', 'Figure 2': 'Histopathologic and immunohistochemical examination findings showing cellular infiltration and staining results'}}}",
